+Core output declarations have a pure owner in `scripts/core-projection-contract.ts`.
+It selects per-file protocol binding scope and native additions, then checks
+declared paths against successful publications. The packager checks for unmatched
+paths after publishing core runtime, preserving failure order. Repeated declarations
+keep their first position and last supplied lines; this is a path-coverage contract,
+not duplicate-declaration or duplicate-output rejection. Platform path normalization
+and filesystem work stay in the packager.
+
+`scripts/harness-runtime-data.ts` selects the identity and declared host fields
+that ship in `tools/data/harness.json`. It creates a fresh record, omits undeclared
+optional fields and retains the manifest's rules-directory choice. The packager
+owns serialization and publication; runtime readers retain their existing
+validation and capability interpretation. Extra manifest keys are not runtime
+configuration, and changing the projection invalidates generated-source freshness.
+
+Root-integration wire compatibility belongs to
+`core/tools/aidlc-root-integration-codec.ts`. Newer recognized policies ship with
+the older `whole-file` base policy and an `extendedPolicy` field. Readers restore
+recognized extensions and otherwise retain the base policy. Existing distribution
+APIs delegate to this pure codec; filesystem admission and reconciliation retain
+their existing owners. The core input fingerprint includes codec changes.
+
+`core/tools/aidlc-projection-path.ts` owns lexical admission of projection metadata
+paths and top-level names. It retains declared spellings and returns a decision;
+the distribution adapter keeps error labels and filesystem/symlink checks. This
+does not add path normalization or change the accepted metadata grammar.
+
+Native agent metadata belongs in the manifest's `frontmatterAdditions`. The
+pure owner in `scripts/agent-surface-projection.ts` validates those additions
+and plans the dispatch document and any inline persona copy from supplied
+content and role facts. The packager loads knowledge and roles, then writes
+the planned surfaces. On Kiro IDE, an inline persona copy omits the native
+permission block; Kiro CLI's `tools` grant alone creates no extra copy.
+Refusals retain the existing diagnostic text and publication order: a persona
+copy can already exist when its dispatch metadata is refused. Publication
+does not roll back earlier files. The projection module is a packaging input,
+so changing its policy invalidates the recorded generated output.
+
+Onboarding placement has its own pure owner in `scripts/onboarding-projection.ts`.
+With `harnessDst`, it keeps the shared document neutral and schedules native
+setup afterward inside the harness tree. Without that destination, it combines
+native setup before the shared guidance in the declared document. The existing
+renderer still owns slots and marker validation; the packager owns reads,
+token projection and ordered publication. A failed native render can leave the
+earlier neutral document, as before. This policy is also a tracked packaging
+input.
+
+Authored host files use `scripts/harness-surface-projection.ts` to select their
+native projection from the declared flavor and source path. Kiro agent JSON
+keeps model selection in the existing core model-policy owner, expands its
+shared-methodology file glob without preloading format documents, and applies
+the manifest's hook suffix afterward. The shared knowledge filenames are read
+only when that exact file glob occurs; explicit resources retain their order
+and values. JSON parsing, missing-file handling and ordered reads/writes stay
+with the packager. This is filesystem knowledge projection; MCP resource
+behavior is unchanged.
+
